    "https://jobs.montana.edu/postings/51504": {
      "title": "Position Details",
      "description": "Montana State University Employment Opportunities | NAGPRA Program Lead Skip to Main Content Jobs at Montana State University Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About MSU About Bozeman Benefits Academics NAGPRA Program Lead Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. This position is not eligible for sponsorship. Position Details Position Information Announcement Number STAFF - VA - 26406 For questions regarding this position, please contact: Alison Harmon harmon@montana.edu 406-994-5053 Classification Title Program/Project Lead Working Title NAGPRA Program Lead Brief Position Overview The NAGPRA Program Leadcoordinates compliance, consultations, repatriations, and other activities associated with ensuring MSU’s compliance with the Native American Graves Protection and Repatriation Act ( NAGPRA ) federal regulation by coordinating inventory and curation of Native American artifacts and remains. This position will work to strengthen MSU’s NAGPRA program and will report directly to the Associate Vice President of Research Compliance. Position Number 4C0105 Department Research Integrity and Compliance Division Office of Research and Economic Development Appointment Type Professional Contract Term Fiscal Year Semester If other, specify From date If other, specify End date FLSA Exempt Union Affiliation Exempt from Collective Bargaining FTE 1.0 Benefits Eligible Eligible Salary Salary starting at $80,000 annually, commensurate with experience, education, and qualifications Contract Type LOA If other, please specify Recruitment Type Open Position Details General Statement The Office of Research Compliance ( ORC ) oversees University programs to ensure compliance with federal, state, and local regulations for research. ORC creates and supports an environment that furthers the ethical and responsible conduct of research. Duties and Responsibilities Primary duties and responsibilities include: Develop and implement a detailed MSU NAGPRA policy, in coordination with the MSU NAGPRA team (team includes representatives from the Museum of the Rockies, the academic departments of Native American Studies, Sociology and Anthropology, History and Philosophy, Earth Sciences, and other units as appropriate), Legal Counsel, and Administration, ensuring compliance with NAGPRA and all applicable laws and policies. Update and manage inventories of ancestral human remains and NAGPRA -eligible objects in MSU collections, compile cultural affiliation documents, and prepare Federal notices and communications. Work closely with Tribal Historic Preservation Officers ( THPO ) and/or their designees to coordinate tribal access to, as well as repatriation and disposition of, human remains and cultural items and archaeological collections in MSU’s custody. Represent MSU as lead NAGPRA staff at regular meetings with tribal government officials and representatives to coordinate compliance, address tribal concerns, and when possible, negotiate culturally sensitive resolutions. Serve as the primary point of contact for all NAGPRA inquiries seeking information about Native American remains and objects falling under NAGPRA regulations. Advance MSU’s NAGPRA standing through attendance at conferences and workshops, service on advisory panels, and holding office or performing relevant committee assignments in professional organizations. Participate in promoting and coordinating outreach activities for research and education. Collaborate and consult closely with the MSU NAGPRA team representatives in matters of compliance and procedure. Develop and submit grant proposals and funding requests to support NAGPRA activities. Administer NAGPRA -related budgets and purchase all necessary materials and supplies. Establish work priorities and supervise all employees assigned to them in support of these tasks. Work with MSU’s Legal Counsel on MOUs for non- NAGPRA repatriation. Required Qualifications – Experience, Education, Knowledge & Skills Master’s degree in Indigenous Studies, Museum Studies, or related field that provided experience with cultural collections management and collaborating with tribal nations on repatriation plans; or three years equivalent experience working with American Indian tribes to repatriate NAGPRA related or other items of cultural significance, or working with descent communities and caring for culturally significant objects; and/or equivalent training, education or experience with traditional Tribal Elders, Tribal Cultural Departments and Historians, or Tribal Historic Preservation Officers. At least one year of experience working with institutions on collections-related ethical and legal commitments, including but not limited to NAGPRA and 36 C.F.R. § 79, “Curation of Federally-Owned and Administered Archeological Collections.” Experience working with archaeological methods, archaeological collections, and culturally sensitive artifacts, including experience handling ancestral remains, funerary objects and other cultural objects. Knowledgeable about the Indigenous communities of Montana, especially with tribes affiliated with different geographical categories i.e. coastal, plains, northern plains, basin, etc. Knowledge of the principles of government-to-government consultation. Preferred Qualifications – Experience, Education, Knowledge & Skills Doctoral degree in Indigenous Studies, Museum Studies, or related field with specialized practice involving relationships and substantive consultation with Tribal Historic Preservation Officers. Demonstrated understanding of, and direct experience with: (a) tribal consultation practices and processes, b) building and initiating positive working relationships with tribes and tribal citizens; and © NAGPRA . Experience working with collections management (or other related cultural heritage field) on projects that are informed and/or designed by Indigenous perspectives. Experience with writing summary and cultural affiliation reports and “Notices of Inventory Completion” and “Notices of Intent to Repatriate” for compliance with NAGPRA . Experience with programmatic agreements. Knowledge of North American Indigenous History, Archaeology, and related ‘Curation Crisis’ History. The Successful Candidate Will Tactful and diplomatic verbal and written communication skills. Excellent organizational management skills. Ability to create inventories and adhere to different tribal requests regarding the care and storage of specific artifacts. Strong skills in program and project management, facilitation and collaboration. Ability to effectively represent and coordinate organizational strategy and administrative initiatives with campus senior level management and external contacts. Demonstrated ability in providing leadership in and contributions to committees and workgroups where problems have to be solved efficiently. Strong ability to build and maintain positive and productive relationships with a broad range of staff, co-workers, tribal citizens and campus stakeholders. Ability to handle highly sensitive and confidential issues that require independent judgment, strict discretion, and confidentiality. Ability to evaluate and synthesize various types of information and perspectives. Position Special Requirements/Additional Information Must possess a valid driver’s license, an acceptable driving record, and meet all State of Montana requirements to operate State/University vehicles. (If successful candidate possesses a valid out-of-state driver’s license, must obtain a valid Montana driver’s license within 60 days of hire.) This job description should not be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities or requirements, but a general description of the job. Nothing contained herein restricts Montana State University’s rights to assign or reassign duties and responsibilities to this job at any time. Physical Demands To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily with or without reasonable accommodations. The requirements listed above are representative of the knowledge, skill, and/or ability required. This position has supervisory duties? Yes Posting Detail Information Number of Vacancies 1 Desired Start Date Upon completion of a successful search Position End Date (if temporary) Open Date Close Date Applications will be: Screening of applications will begin on May 28, 2026 ; however, applications will continue to be accepted until an adequate applicant pool has been established. Special Instructions This position is not eligible for sponsorship. EEO Statement Montana State University is an equal opportunity employer. MSU does not discriminate against any applicant on the basis of race, color, religion, creed, political ideas, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, age, marital status, national origin, physical or mental disability, or any other protected class status in violation of any applicable law. In compliance with the Montana Veteran’s Employment Preference Act, MSU provides preference in employment to veterans, disabled veterans, and certain eligible relatives of veterans. To claim veteran’s preference, please complete the veteran’s preference information located in the Demographics section of your profile. Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). University Human Resources 920 Technology BLVD, Suite A Bozeman, MT 59718 406-994-3651 humanresources@montana.edu Accessibility | Contact List | Directories | Jobs | Legal & Privacy Policy | Site Index Montana State University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.montana.edu/postings/52746": {
      "title": "Training and Development Specialist",
      "description": "Montana State University Employment Opportunities | Training and Development Specialist Skip to Main Content Jobs at Montana State University Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About MSU About Bozeman Benefits Academics Training and Development Specialist Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. This position is not eligible for new sponsorship. Position Details Position Information Announcement Number STAFF - VA - 26419 For questions regarding this position, please contact: Sophia Bielsky Email: sophia.bielsky@montana.edu Phone: (406) 994-5801 Classification Title Training/Dev Coord Working Title Training and Development Specialist Brief Position Overview MSU Access and Success is seeking a Training and Development Specialist to support campus-wide initiatives that foster inclusive dialogue, effective communication, and community engagement. This position plays a key role in developing and delivering educational programming that enhances the ability of students, faculty, and staff to engage across differences and contribute to a welcoming and inclusive campus environment. The Training and Development Specialist manages several signature programs, including Safe Zone, and Cultural Bridges, and collaborates with campus partners to expand training capacity and impact. The Training and Development Specialist is responsible for the design, implementation, and continuous improvement of educational programs that support dialogue across differences, inclusive practices, and community engagement. This position manages key training initiatives and collaborates with campus partners to expand learning opportunities that foster effective communication, cultural awareness, and inclusive excellence. Position Number TBD Department DISC Division Division of Student Success Appointment Type Classified Contract Term Fiscal Year Semester If other, specify From date If other, specify End date FLSA Non-Exempt Union Affiliation FOCUS-MFPE FTE 1.0 Benefits Eligible Eligible Salary Starting at $23.323 per hour, commensurate with experience, education, and qualifications Contract Type Classified Salary If other, please specify Recruitment Type Open Position Details General Statement Duties and Responsibilities Curriculum Development & Assessment Design, develop, assess, and regularly revise workshop curricula and facilitator training materials for the Skills to Dialogue (S2D) program, Safe Zone program, and Cultural Bridges training series, incorporating participant feedback and emerging best practices. Adapt training content to meet specific departmental or campus needs in collaboration with the Director. Ensure programs align with Montana State University’s mission and commitment to student success and inclusive excellence. Develop and implement assessment tools to evaluate the effectiveness of training programs Analyze the data and feedback to continuously improve and adapt programs to meet the needs of the campus community Partnership Development & Outreach Build and maintain partnerships with academic and administrative units across campus who are interested in training content related to dialogue, inclusive practices, and communicating across differences. Collaborate with campus stakeholders to identify training needs and co-create workshops focused on identity, communication, and inclusion. Expand Cultural Bridges offerings to address a wide range of community perspectives and experiences. Program Management & Coordination Oversee and manage the Safe Zone program and Cultural Bridges training series. Conduct engaging, skill-focused, informative presentations, workshops, events, and training sessions for students, faculty, and staff. Facilitate discussions and activities that promote understanding and skill-building in areas such as inclusive practice and dialogue Work with partners to coordinate logistical elements for trainings and workshops, including scheduling, room reservations, catering, facilitator assignments, communications, and materials preparation. Recruit facilitators, plan and execute facilitator trainings for all training programs. Develop and implement participant follow-up strategies, including organizing student dialogue sessions and ongoing engagement opportunities. Work with campus partners to fulfill training requests and design custom curriculum to meet their needs, as possible Facilitator Recruitment & Development Develop strategies to expand program reach and training capacity. Recruit, train, and support a diverse cohort of student and staff facilitators. Provide ongoing mentorship, coaching, and evaluation for facilitators. Create an initial and ongoing training program to educate facilitators about facilitation best practices, training content, and creating space for participants to develop and practice their skills. Marketing & Communications Develop program messaging, branding, and communication strategies. Provide guidance and direction to student staff or partners on marketing materials and outreach efforts. Promote programs to increase participation and awareness across the university community. Collaboration & Special Projects Work closely with the Director on strategic training initiatives and custom workshop requests. Contribute to program assessment, reporting, and continuous improvement efforts. Required Qualifications – Experience, Education, Knowledge & Skills Bachelor’s degree in education, student affairs, communication, social sciences, or a related field or an equivalent combination of relevant education and experience. Experience facilitating trainings, workshops, or educational programs. Experience developing curriculum or training materials related to dialogue, inclusion, or communication. Demonstrated experience in program coordination or project management. Excellent interpersonal, written, and verbal communication skills. Demonstrated ability to work effectively with diverse populations, including students, faculty, and staff. Preferred Qualifications – Experience, Education, Knowledge & Skills Master’s degree in higher education, student affairs, organizational development, or a related field. Experience in a higher education setting. Knowledge of intergroup dialogue, conflict resolution, or inclusive pedagogy frameworks. Experience with program assessment and data-informed decision-making. Familiarity with marketing, outreach strategies, or supervising student staff. The Successful Candidate Will Be open to receiving feedback and adjusting accordingly Adapt communication style and language to diverse audiences Deliver clear, concise, and actionable instructions Facilitate productive disagreement and surface differing perspectives Skillfully hold silence, tension, and emotion without prematurely resolving it Demonstrate intellectual humility, embracing learning, unlearning, and making mistakes Exercise strong judgment Model accountability and follow through on commitments Collaborate effectively, building trust and sharing credit with integrity Adapt quickly and effectively when plans shift Manage timelines, logistics, and competing priorities with discipline Influence and guide through credibility, relationships, and facilitation rather than authority. Position Special Requirements/Additional Information This job description should not be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities or requirements, but a general description of the job. Nothing contained herein restricts Montana State University’s rights to assign or reassign duties and responsibilities to this job at any time. Physical Demands To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily with or without reasonable accommodations. The requirements listed above are representative of the knowledge, skill, and/or ability required. This position has supervisory duties? No Posting Detail Information Number of Vacancies 1 Desired Start Date Upon completion of a successful search. Position End Date (if temporary) Open Date Close Date Applications will be: Review of applications will be on a continual basis and will continue until the position(s) are filled. Special Instructions This position is not eligible for new sponsorship. EEO Statement Montana State University is an equal opportunity employer. MSU does not discriminate against any applicant on the basis of race, color, religion, creed, political ideas, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, age, marital status, national origin, physical or mental disability, or any other protected class status in violation of any applicable law. In compliance with the Montana Veteran’s Employment Preference Act, MSU provides preference in employment to veterans, disabled veterans, and certain eligible relatives of veterans. To claim veteran’s preference, please complete the veteran’s preference information located in the Demographics section of your profile. Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). University Human Resources 920 Technology BLVD, Suite A Bozeman, MT 59718 406-994-3651 humanresources@montana.edu Accessibility | Contact List | Directories | Jobs | Legal & Privacy Policy | Site Index Montana State University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.montana.edu/postings/50036": {
      "title": "Chinese Non-Tenure Track Faculty (Applicant Pool)",
      "description": "Montana State University Employment Opportunities | Chinese Non-Tenure Track Faculty (Applicant Pool) Skip to Main Content Jobs at Montana State University Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About MSU About Bozeman Benefits Academics Chinese Non-Tenure Track Faculty (Applicant Pool) Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. This position is not eligible for NEW sponsorship. Instructors teach 1 to 5 language classes per semester. All positions are pending adequate enrollment and funding. This advertisement is for an applicant pool. It does not indicate whether or not we currently have an open position. When an opening occurs, review of applications will be on a continual basis and will continue until the position(s) are filled. Applicants will be notified if they are considered for a position. Position Details Position Information Announcement Number FAC - VA - 26104 For questions regarding this position, please contact: Galen Brokaw Department Head (406) 994-6448 brokaw@montana.edu Classification Title Instructor Working Title Chinese Non-Tenure Track Faculty (Applicant Pool) Brief Position Overview Department of Modern Languages and Literatures maintains an applicant pool for Non-Tenure Track Instructors in Chinese. Instructors typically teach 1 to 5 classes per semester. All positions are pending adequate enrollment and funding. This advertisement is for an applicant pool. It does not indicate whether or not there are any current openings. Qualified candidates will be contacted in the event a position opens. Faculty Tenure Track No Faculty Rank Non-Tenure Track Position Number 4A6151 Department Modern Languages & Literature Division College of Letters & Science Appointment Type Non-Tenure Track (Pooled) Contract Term Other Semester Fall & Spring If other, specify From date If other, specify End date Union Affiliation Contact HR for Determination FTE 0.10 to 1.0 Benefits Eligible Depends on FTE (please contact Human Resources - 406-994-3651) Salary Salary commensurate with experience, education, and qualifications Contract Type LOA If other, please specify Recruitment Type Open Position Details General Statement Montana State University seeks qualified individuals to provide high-quality instruction and to perform related academic duties as needed. Instructional assignments are contingent upon departmental needs and the availability of funding. Applications are accepted on a continual basis (unless otherwise indicated) and will be maintained in the pool for up to two years. After two years, candidates will be notified to resubmit their application materials for continued consideration. Application materials will be reviewed by the appropriate search committee or hiring authority, and applicants will be notified if they are being considered for an open position. Non-tenure-track appointments may be part-time or full-time and vary in duration depending on the specific needs of the department or program. Salary is dependent on the number of courses taught and the duration of the appointment. Duties and Responsibilities Instructors typically teach 1 to 5 classes per semester. All positions are pending adequate enrollment and funding. Required Qualifications – Experience, Education, Knowledge & Skills A Bachelor’s degree or equivalent from a Foreign University; and A Master’s degree or equivalent from a Foreign University in Chinese or a closely related field, or relevant knowledge and experience appropriate to the position (e.g., superior level proficiency; extensive residence abroad, etc.); and Proficiency level in Chinese appropriate to the position. Preferred Qualifications – Experience, Education, Knowledge & Skills PhD in Chinese or other relevant discipline. Superior level proficiency in Chinese. Experience teaching at the college level. The Successful Candidate Will Have effective interpersonal skills, including the ability to communicate successfully and maintain cooperative working relationships with faculty, staff, and students. Position Special Requirements/Additional Information The position for which you have applied MAY BE a Union position. Union affiliation is determined by job duties and the collective bargaining unit. Non-Tenure Track Faculty – Any position that is .5 FTE or more is represented by the Associated Faculty of Montana State University ( AFMSU ). Faculty covered by AFMSU may choose to pay membership dues. This is a condition of employment. Additional information can be found at AFMSU or by emailing AFSMU . Any position less than .5 FTE is not represented by the Union and is not required to pay dues. If the FTE changes during employment, the representational status of this position could be impacted. Exceptions: Anyone having successfully completed a PEI exam or any faculty working in Extension are exempt from the Union. This job description should not be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities or requirements, but a general description of the job. Nothing contained herein restricts Montana State University’s rights to assign or reassign duties and responsibilities to this job at any time. Physical Demands To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily with or without reasonable accommodations. The requirements listed above are representative of the knowledge, skill, and/or ability required. The Program The Department Modern Languages & Literatures offers courses in Arabic, Chinese, Japanese, French, German, and Spanish. Students may major or minor in Asian Studies, French & Francophone Studies, German Studies, Hispanic Studies, and Latin American & Latino Studies. Teaching options in French, German, and Spanish are also offered. The College The College of Letters & Science ( CLS ), the largest center for learning, teaching and research at Montana State University, offers students an excellent liberal arts and sciences education in nearly 50 majors, 25 minors and over 25 graduate degrees within the four areas of the humanities, natural sciences, mathematical, and social sciences. Building on the great strength of our traditional disciplines, the college provides exciting opportunities for faculty and students at the cutting edge of interdisciplinary inquiry. CLS is dedicated to supporting student success, discovering and disseminating new knowledge, and providing an atmosphere in which discovery and learning are integrated and valued. CLS is at the confluence of intellectual tradition, discovery, and innovation. Posting Detail Information Number of Vacancies Multiple Positions Desired Start Date Fall and Spring semesters Position End Date (if temporary) Open Date Close Date Applications will be: Applications will be accepted and screened for the duration of this pool. Special Instructions This position is not eligible for NEW sponsorship. Instructors teach 1 to 5 language classes per semester. All positions are pending adequate enrollment and funding. This advertisement is for an applicant pool. It does not indicate whether or not we currently have an open position. When an opening occurs, review of applications will be on a continual basis and will continue until the position(s) are filled. Applicants will be notified if they are considered for a position. EEO Statement Montana State University is an equal opportunity employer. MSU does not discriminate against any applicant on the basis of race, color, religion, creed, political ideas, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, age, marital status, national origin, physical or mental disability, or any other protected class status in violation of any applicable law. In compliance with the Montana Veteran’s Employment Preference Act, MSU provides preference in employment to veterans, disabled veterans, and certain eligible relatives of veterans. To claim veteran’s preference, please complete the veteran’s preference information located in the Demographics section of your profile. FLSA Details FLSA Determination FLSA Status Exempt Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter addressing each of the Required and Preferred Qualifications (max 2 pages) Curriculum Vitae Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). University Human Resources 920 Technology BLVD, Suite A Bozeman, MT 59718 406-994-3651 humanresources@montana.edu Accessibility | Contact List | Directories | Jobs | Legal & Privacy Policy | Site Index Montana State University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.montana.edu/postings/52484": {
      "title": "Executive Director, QCORE",
      "description": "Montana State University Employment Opportunities | Executive Director, QCORE Skip to Main Content Jobs at Montana State University Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About MSU About Bozeman Benefits Academics Executive Director, QCORE Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. This position is not eligible for sponsorship. Preferred start by January 11, 2027 Applicants will be required to list a minimum of five references during the application process. Position Details Position Information Announcement Number STAFF - VA - 27046 For questions regarding this position, please contact: William G. Thomas III Dean, College of Letters & Science 406-994-4288 william.thomas22@montana.edu Classification Title Executive Director Working Title Executive Director, QCORE Brief Position Overview QCORE seeks a strategic leader to build and sustain an organization that advances research and scientific discovery. Position Number 4C0093 Department Physics Division College of Letters & Science Appointment Type Professional Contract Term Fiscal Year Semester If other, specify From date If other, specify End date FLSA Exempt Union Affiliation Exempt from Collective Bargaining FTE 1.0 Benefits Eligible Eligible Salary Salary commensurate with experience, education, and qualifications. Contract Type LOA If other, please specify Recruitment Type Open Position Details General Statement The Executive Director of QCORE serves as the head of a university-wide research institute and works closely with affiliated faculty and staff to build and lead a successful program advancing research and scientific discovery in quantum science, engineering, and business. QCORE’s primary objective is to empower Montana’s quantum ecosystem by uniting cutting-edge research, industry partnerships, and community engagement – enabling breakthrough technologies that shape the future of quantum innovation. The vision of QCORE is to lead Montana’s transformation into a global powerhouse for quantum-enabled technologies by 2035, fostering economic growth, academic excellence and a resilient, future-focused workforce. QCORE exists to steward the development of transformative quantum technologies in service to the common good. QCORE will help students plan for careers in quantum and quantum-enabling systems and will support transitioning into those careers through strategic partnerships with Montana’s growing quantum industry. Strategic partnerships serve as the mechanism for QCORE to strengthen relationships between industry, research and innovators – growing collaboration and creating shared opportunities for beneficial technology innovations. Pending approval of research center status, QCORE is housed in The College of Letters & Science ( CLS ), the largest center for learning, teaching and research at Montana State University. CLS offers students an excellent liberal arts and sciences education in nearly 50 majors, 25 minors and over 25 graduate degrees within the four areas of the humanities, natural sciences, mathematical, and social sciences. Building on the great strength of our traditional disciplines, the college provides exciting opportunities for faculty and students at the cutting edge of interdisciplinary inquiry. CLS is dedicated to supporting student success, discovering and disseminating new knowledge, and providing an atmosphere in which discovery and learning are integrated and valued. CLS is at the confluence of intellectual tradition, discovery, and innovation. When research center status is finalized for QCORE , the center will be in the Office of the Vice President for Research and Economic Development. Duties and Responsibilities Strategically position QCORE to be a nationally recognized, best-in-class research and development center for advancement of quantum technology, a leader in quantum workforce development and a deeply integrated innovation ecosystem that supports the translation of quantum and related technologies to industry. Develop and implement long-term strategies to improve QCORE’s overall sustainability, effectiveness, and responsiveness to the needs of sponsoring organizations and companies in the state, region, nation, and internationally. Develop and maintain a best-in-class governance structure for QCORE . Build and nurture long-term relationships with key stakeholders, including federal agencies, individual donors, institutional funders, congressional representatives, and corporate partners. Build strategic alliances and partnerships with other testbeds, academic institutions, corporations, and governments to expand QCORE’s reach, visibility, and impact. Lead efforts to secure sustainable funding through partnerships, campaigns, and new initiatives to diversify the organization’s revenue (e.g., federal and private grants, federal cooperative awards, donations, industrial partnerships, social enterprises). This includes engagement with our legislature. Participate in sector-specific organizations relevant to quantum technologies and quantum supply chain, including standards development and testbed communities of practice. Present at national and international gatherings of experts in these respective fields. Leads efforts to ensure that QCORE remains a vital part of MSU’s land-grant mission, promoting quantum science and economic development in Montana, the region, and nation, and providing practical learning opportunities for students. Support QCORE faculty and scientists seeking and obtaining external funding to promote innovation, discovery, and dissemination of quantum science and technology. Ensure operational excellence, overseeing all aspects of budgeting, accounting, personnel, facilities, security, and safety. Ensure that QCORE complies with all legal, regulatory, safety, and ethical standards at local, national, and international levels. Ensure that QCORE meets all required grant reporting requirements. Coordinate with QCORE’s leadership team to lead and mentor staff, fostering a positive, collaborative, and mission-driven organizational culture. Oversee the monitoring and evaluation of program outcomes to measure success and inform future strategies at a national and international level. Prioritize sustainability in all programs and initiatives, ensuring long-term environmental, social, and financial viability. Required Qualifications – Experience, Education, Knowledge & Skills PhD in Physics, Engineering, or a related field, or a terminal degree with equivalent industry experience. Demonstrated experience in strategic leadership of a research center or related entity, or business. Demonstrated experience in federal research grant-seeking, fundraising, or business development. Preferred Qualifications – Experience, Education, Knowledge & Skills A demonstrated record of scientific or business innovation. Demonstrated experience with technology transfer. Experience navigating or contributing to the mission of a land-grant university, including economic development, outreach, and workforce training. The Successful Candidate Will Have knowledge of the disciplines and programs represented in QCORE and a commitment to our vision. A record of positive staff and personnel relations and interactions. A creative and approachable manner that kindles the innovative spirit of others. A strong ability to communicate effectively as exemplified by the candidates’ writing, speaking, and listening skills. Demonstrated accomplishments in research, scholarship, and innovative partnerships. Position Special Requirements/Additional Information This position is contingent upon continued grant funding. This is a full-time position with extensive travel required for regional, national, and international events and partnership meetings. Occasional evening and weekend work may be required to support onsite event management. Successful candidate must be able to comply with the federally mandated requirements of U.S. export control laws, which may require proof that candidate is a U.S. citizen. All QCORE employees may be required to meet NIST 800-53 requirements for access to Controlled Unclassified Information ( CUI ). This position may require the ability to obtain a DoD Security Clearance. This job description should not be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities or requirements, but a general description of the job. Nothing contained herein restricts Montana State University’s rights to assign or reassign duties and responsibilities to this job at any time. Physical Demands To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily with or without reasonable accommodation. The requirements listed above are representative of the knowledge, skill, and/or ability required. This position has supervisory duties? Yes Posting Detail Information Number of Vacancies 1 Desired Start Date At the conclusion of a successful search Position End Date (if temporary) Open Date Close Date Applications will be: Screening of applications will begin on September 14, 2026 ; however, applications will continue to be accepted until an adequate applicant pool has been established. Special Instructions This position is not eligible for sponsorship. Preferred start by January 11, 2027 Applicants will be required to list a minimum of five references during the application process. EEO Statement Montana State University is an equal opportunity employer. MSU does not discriminate against any applicant on the basis of race, color, religion, creed, political ideas, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, age, marital status, national origin, physical or mental disability, or any other protected class status in violation of any applicable law. In compliance with the Montana Veteran’s Employment Preference Act, MSU provides preference in employment to veterans, disabled veterans, and certain eligible relatives of veterans. To claim veteran’s preference, please complete the veteran’s preference information located in the Demographics section of your profile. Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). University Human Resources 920 Technology BLVD, Suite A Bozeman, MT 59718 406-994-3651 humanresources@montana.edu Accessibility | Contact List | Directories | Jobs | Legal & Privacy Policy | Site Index Montana State University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.montana.edu/postings/52199": {
      "title": "Pharmacy Technician",
      "description": "Montana State University Employment Opportunities | Pharmacy Technician Skip to Main Content Jobs at Montana State University Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About MSU About Bozeman Benefits Academics Pharmacy Technician Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Announcement Number STAFF - VA - 26424 For questions regarding this position, please contact: Whitney Milhoan Director Student Health Services 406-994-2311 whitney.milhoan@montana.edu Classification Title Pharmacy Tech Working Title Pharmacy Technician Brief Position Overview Join a mission-driven healthcare team dedicated to MSU student well-being. The SHS Pharmacy Technician plays a key role in delivering accurate, confidential, and accessible pharmacy services to MSU students from a state-of-the-art campus facility. Position Number 4M0686 Department SWC Pharmacy Division Division of Student Success Appointment Type Classified Contract Term Fiscal Year Semester If other, specify From date If other, specify End date FLSA Non-Exempt Union Affiliation FOCUS-MFPE FTE 1.0 Benefits Eligible Eligible Salary $23.00 per hour, commensurate with experience, education, and qualifications Contract Type Classified Hourly If other, please specify Recruitment Type Open Position Details General Statement MSU Student Health Services serves the diverse health needs of the MSU student body through an integrated model designed to reduce barriers to care. The SHS Pharmacy operates within the Student Wellness Center and serves as a critical component of the campus healthcare ecosystem. This position reports to the Pharmacy Manager and works under the direct supervision of licensed pharmacists. Duties and Responsibilities Process and dispense prescriptions under pharmacist supervision in compliance with Montana Board of Pharmacy regulations Strictly maintain patient confidentiality and HIPAA compliance at all times Deliver courteous, professional service to MSU students Manage medication inventory including controlled substances Process insurance claims and billing Over-the-counter and retail sale management Maintain required records and support pharmacy administrative functions Required Qualifications – Experience, Education, Knowledge & Skills Active Montana Certified Pharmacy Technician license (CPhT via PTCB or ExCPT/ NHA ), or eligibility for a provisional license; active licensure required prior to start date ( ARM 24.174.701) High school diploma or equivalent Must be at least 18 years of age (Montana Board of Pharmacy requirement) Preferred Qualifications – Experience, Education, Knowledge & Skills CPhT credential ( PTCB preferred) Previous experience in a retail, clinic, or hospital pharmacy setting Experience with pharmacy management software Familiarity with third-party insurance billing and claims processing Experience working in a college health, public health, or student-facing service environment The Successful Candidate Will Monday–Friday availability during SHS Pharmacy operating hours Strong attention to detail and commitment to accuracy in a high-stakes clinical environment Demonstrated understanding of and commitment to patient privacy and HIPAA compliance Effective communication and interpersonal skills; ability to interact respectfully with a diverse student population Ability to manage competing priorities and maintain workflow in a busy pharmacy setting Professional, ethical conduct and reliability Position Special Requirements/Additional Information Montana Board of Pharmacy Compliance: This position is subject to all applicable provisions of Montana Administrative Rules Chapter 24.174. The selected candidate must maintain an active Montana pharmacy technician license and comply with all continuing education requirements mandated by the certifying agency for annual license renewal. This job description should not be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities, or requirements, but a general description of the job. Nothing contained herein restricts Montana State University’s rights to assign or reassign duties and responsibilities to this job at any time. Availability: Monday–Friday availability is required. Position may be offered on a year-round or academic year basis as noted in the specific position announcement. Physical Demands To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily with or without reasonable accommodations. Requirements include: ability to stand for extended periods; manual dexterity to count, label, and package medications; ability to lift and carry pharmacy supplies (up to 25 lbs). The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill, and/or ability required. This position has supervisory duties? No Posting Detail Information Number of Vacancies 1 Desired Start Date Upon completion of a successful search. Position End Date (if temporary) Open Date Close Date Applications will be: Screening of applications will begin on June 12th 2026 ; however, applications will continue to be accepted until an adequate applicant pool has been established. Special Instructions EEO Statement Montana State University is an equal opportunity employer. MSU does not discriminate against any applicant on the basis of race, color, religion, creed, political ideas, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, age, marital status, national origin, physical or mental disability, or any other protected class status in violation of any applicable law. In compliance with the Montana Veteran’s Employment Preference Act, MSU provides preference in employment to veterans, disabled veterans, and certain eligible relatives of veterans. To claim veteran’s preference, please complete the veteran’s preference information located in the Demographics section of your profile. Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). University Human Resources 920 Technology BLVD, Suite A Bozeman, MT 59718 406-994-3651 humanresources@montana.edu Accessibility | Contact List | Directories | Jobs | Legal & Privacy Policy | Site Index Montana State University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.montana.edu/postings/52813": {
      "title": "Position Details",
      "description": "Montana State University Employment Opportunities | Laborer Skip to Main Content Jobs at Montana State University Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About MSU About Bozeman Benefits Academics Laborer Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. This position is not eligible for new sponsorship. Position Details Position Information Announcement Number STAFF - VA - 27078 For questions regarding this position, please contact: Amber Gregg amber.gregg1@montana.edu 406-994-5454 Classification Title Equip Oper I Working Title Laborer Brief Position Overview Under general supervision of the Campus Maintenance Manager, the Laborer provides services to the University by performing, assisting and supporting the maintenance and care of buildings, grounds, equipment, and utilities. Position Number 4N1332 Department Facility Services Division University Services Appointment Type Classified Contract Term Fiscal Year Semester If other, specify From date If other, specify End date FLSA Non-Exempt Union Affiliation Teamsters FTE 1.0 Benefits Eligible Eligible Salary $24.25 per hour, commensurate with experience, education, and qualifications Contract Type Classified Hourly If other, please specify Recruitment Type Open Position Details General Statement Facilities Services is charged with the maintenance and operations of all university buildings, grounds, and infrastructure systems. Facilities comprised of the four service management areas of Environmental Services, Campus Maintenance, Engineering and Utilities, and General Operations, as well as the Business Management and Administrative areas necessary to support the Facilities operation. The Campus Maintenance section of Facilities Services is responsible for operations, maintenance, repair, renovation, and construction of buildings, grounds, equipment, and utilities for all university departments. Under general supervision of the Campus Maintenance Manager, the Laborer is responsible for a variety of technical and manual work supporting the operations, maintenance, repair, renovation, and construction of buildings, grounds, streets, walkways, equipment, and utilities for Montana State University. Duties and Responsibilities Perform support work and provide assistance to other trades (plumbers, electricians, painters, carpenters, etc.) including site preparation, delivery of supplies, materials and equipment, and site cleanup. Perform and assist with a variety of skilled technical work (e.g. masonry, asbestos abatement and mold remediation, demolition, concrete, construction site preparation, etc.). Operate a variety of equipment including manual, power, and heavy equipment (e.g. bucket truck, forklift, Grad-All, loaders, dump truck, roll-off trucks, snow removal equipment, pressure washers, chainsaws, etc.) to perform campus operational, renovation, construction, and maintenance work. Operates vehicles and specialized equipment, such as trucks, GradAll, forklift, snow removal equipment, Roll off trucks, loaders, backhoes, and mowers in support of renovation, construction, repair, and maintenance of campus buildings, streets, and grounds. Inspect, repair, and perform preventative maintenance on roof membranes and roof drainage systems to prevent damage from water and weather exposure. Institute necessary steps to confine hazards and/or protect building occupants when suspected hazardous materials are encountered on any maintenance, alteration, repair or renovation job in progress including abating small areas of asbestos or mold containing materials using established removal policies and procedures. Perform and assist with snow removal operations, waste management operations (landfill, recycling, e-waste), stormwater compliance operations. Support large campus activities like sports, concerts, and other significant University events. Required Qualifications – Experience, Education, Knowledge & Skills High school graduation or an equivalent combination of education and/or experience . Progressively responsible experience in general construction laborer experience that includes facility maintenance and construction equipment operation, or an equivalent combination of relevant education and experience. Demonstrated experience operating heavy equipment such as forklifts, trucks, bucket trucks, etc. and using power tools. Demonstrated experience using general construction equipment and hand tools including masonry tools, ladders, scaffolds, chainsaws, etc. Demonstrated experience providing a high level of customer service in a fast-paced environment, continuously displaying a positive, professional and helpful manner. Preferred Qualifications – Experience, Education, Knowledge & Skills Possess a valid asbestos/mold removal certification. If a successful candidate does not currently possess an asbestos/mold certification, it must be obtained within one year of hire. Successful experience with the maintenance and repair of single ply roofing systems. Working knowledge of OSHA laws and regulations, and asbestos and mold abatement methods and procedures. Experience operating snow removal machinery. Valid Commercial Driver’s License (Class B, Type II). The Successful Candidate Will Consistently exercise initiative and good judgment. Effectively and safely operate work-related equipment and tools. Work proficiently in the use of a computerized maintenance management system, e-mail, and other computer applications as assigned or required. Accurately follow written and oral instructions and procedures. Communicate effectively with co-workers, supervisors and clients both in person and over the phone. Establish and maintain effective working relationships with supervisors, coworkers and the public. Be a successful team player. Will establish and maintain effective working relations with co-workers and the public in a team-oriented environment. Have demonstrated competence accurately following instructions, procedures, and regulations; and represent the department and MSU to others in a professional and courteous manner. Position Special Requirements/Additional Information Must possess a valid driver’s license, an acceptable driving record, and meet all State of Montana requirements to operate State/University vehicles. (If successful candidate possesses a valid out-of-state driver’s license, must obtain a valid Montana driver’s license within 60 days of hire.) Must possess a valid asbestos/mold removal certification within one year of hire. The final candidate selected for this position will be required to successfully pass a background check. This job description should not be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities or requirements, but a general description of the job. Nothing contained herein restricts Montana State University’s rights to assign or reassign duties and responsibilities to this job at any time. Our benefits package includes: Medical / Dental / Vision Retirement Tuition waiver Annual and sick leave Holiday pay Physical Demands This position requires the ability to: position self to work in and move objects from low positions, in tight spaces, along scaffolds, stairs, roofs, and ladders; and to traverse long distances. Position requires the transport of heavy materials and equipment. Work areas include roofs, poles, manholes, overhead hatches and other difficult access locations. Working conditions include but are not limited to exposure to temperature extremes and inclement weather; working in/around fumes, dust, noise, and research-related activities; and working around equipment, machinery, and site conditions typical of a construction environment. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily with or without reasonable accommodation. The requirements listed above are representative of the knowledge, skill, and/or ability required. This position has supervisory duties? No Posting Detail Information Number of Vacancies 1 Desired Start Date Upon completion of a successful search. Position End Date (if temporary) Open Date Close Date Applications will be: Screening of applications will begin on September 17, 2026 ; however, applications will continue to be accepted until an adequate applicant pool has been established. Special Instructions This position is not eligible for new sponsorship. EEO Statement Montana State University is an equal opportunity employer. MSU does not discriminate against any applicant on the basis of race, color, religion, creed, political ideas, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, age, marital status, national origin, physical or mental disability, or any other protected class status in violation of any applicable law. In compliance with the Montana Veteran’s Employment Preference Act, MSU provides preference in employment to veterans, disabled veterans, and certain eligible relatives of veterans. To claim veteran’s preference, please complete the veteran’s preference information located in the Demographics section of your profile. Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). University Human Resources 920 Technology BLVD, Suite A Bozeman, MT 59718 406-994-3651 humanresources@montana.edu Accessibility | Contact List | Directories | Jobs | Legal & Privacy Policy | Site Index Montana State University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.montana.edu/postings/26751": {
      "title": "Ecology Non-Tenure Track Faculty (Applicant Pool)",
      "description": "Montana State University Employment Opportunities | Ecology Non-Tenure Track Faculty (Applicant Pool) Skip to Main Content Jobs at Montana State University Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About MSU About Bozeman Benefits Academics Ecology Non-Tenure Track Faculty (Applicant Pool) Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Review of applications will be on a continual basis and will continue until the position(s) are filled. Applicants will be notified if their application is being retained in the hiring pool for up to two years. Position Details Position Information Announcement Number FAC - VA - 18054 For questions regarding this position, please contact: Bernadette Diaz, Search Manager Bernadette.diaz@montana.edu 406-994-4481 Classification Title Instructor Working Title Ecology Non-Tenure Track Faculty (Applicant Pool) Brief Position Overview Our instructors teach courses to students that are focusing their education on Fish and Wildlife Management, Conservation Biology, Organismal Biology and Biological Sciences Education. Most courses taught in the department involve field trip components in the Greater Yellowstone Eco-system. The Ecology Department comprises the largest student population within MSU’s College of Letters and Sciences. Faculty Tenure Track No Faculty Rank Non-Tenure Track Position Number 4A6153 Department Ecology Division College of Letters & Science Appointment Type Non-Tenure Track (Pooled) Contract Term Semester Semester Fall or Spring If other, specify From date If other, specify End date Union Affiliation Contact HR for Determination FTE Varies Benefits Eligible Depends on FTE (please contact Human Resources - 406-994-3651) Salary Salary commensurate with experience, education, and qualifications, education, and qualifications Contract Type LOA If other, please specify Recruitment Type Open Position Details General Statement Montana State University seeks qualified individuals to provide non-tenurable level instruction and/or related functions. Instructors are needed to teach classes depending upon need and available funding. Applications are accepted on a continual basis (unless otherwise indicated) and will be maintained in pool for two years. After two years, candidates will be notified to re-submit materials for continued consideration.* The department or program search committees or hiring authority will review and evaluate the submitted application materials. Applicants will be notified if their application is being retained in the non-tenure track faculty hiring pool. Appointments are on a part-time basis and vary in duration, a couple weeks, a semester or an academic year, depending on the specific needs of the department/program. Salary is dependent on number of courses taught and duration. We seek individuals who work professionally and effectively with diverse individuals, possess effective interpersonal, written and verbal communication skills and have the ability to contribute positively to the academic success of the students and the mission of Montana State University. Duties and Responsibilities We hope to attract applicants who can teach in a diverse University community and have demonstrated ability in helping students from diverse backgrounds succeed. Teaching on-campus or online courses. Advising, evaluating and assisting students. Curriculum and/or course development. Participating in weekly faculty meetings. Meeting regularly with, collaborating with, and evaluating peer leader, if one is assigned to your section. Required Qualifications – Experience, Education, Knowledge & Skills Master’s Degree in a relevant field with academic background commensurate with teaching one or more of the specialty areas listed above. Demonstrated experience working with and engaging diverse students and colleagues within and outside the department. Preferred Qualifications – Experience, Education, Knowledge & Skills Demonstrated experience teaching interdisciplinary courses. Demonstrated knowledge of college student development. Demonstrated commitment to student, faculty, and staff diversity. The Successful Candidate Will Have the ability to relate to freshmen and to develop classroom rapport with students Posses the ability to mentor and inspire a diverse student body Have effective interpersonal skills including the ability to collaborate successfully with faculty and staff Possess excellent written and oral communication skills Be skilled in utilizing engaging pedagogy that involves students in the learning process Take initiative and work well independently Have the potential to provide leadership and to assume responsibility for organizational and program activities Have an appreciation of diverse constituencies and add intellectual diversity to the department Be dedicated to supporting and enhancing Montana State University’s initiative for diversity, equity and inclusiveness Position Special Requirements/Additional Information The position for which you have applied MAY BE a Union position. Union affiliation is determined by job duties and the collective bargaining unit. Non-Tenure Track Faculty – Any position that is .5 FTE or more is represented by the Associated Faculty of Montana State University ( AFMSU ). Faculty covered by AFMSU must begin paying either membership dues or representation fees within thirty (30) days of hire. This is a condition of employment. Additional information can be found at AFMSU or by emailing AFSMU . Any position less than .5 FTE is not represented by the Union and is not required to pay dues. If the FTE changes during employment, the representational status of this position could be impacted. Exceptions: Anyone having successfully completed a PEI exam or any faculty working in Extension are exempt from the Union This job description should not be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities or requirements, but a general description of the job. Nothing contained herein restricts Montana State University’s rights to assign or reassign duties and responsibilities to this job at any time. Physical Demands To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily with or without reasonable accommodations. The requirements listed above are representative of the knowledge, skill, and/or ability required. The Program The Department The Department of Ecology provides undergraduate and graduate education and research that advance our understanding of the interactions between living organisms and their environments. This ecological knowledge is critical to the conservation and management of the state’s resources, including fish, wildlife, plants and the ecosystems and landscapes that sustain them. Montana’s natural resources are highly diverse and valuable to the state; thus, our department’s contributions have major impacts on many complex issues. The Ecology Department’s teaching and research addresses critical ecological and natural resources issues for Montana, but also tackles fundamental and applied questions around the globe. Undergraduate programs within the department include Fish & Wildlife Management and Ecology, Conservation Biology and Ecology, Organismal Biology, and Biology Teaching. Graduate programs (M.S. and Ph.D.) include Fish & Wildlife Management or Biology and Biological Sciences and an intercollege Ph.D. in Ecology and Environmental Sciences. The College The College of Letters and Science (L&S), the largest center for learning, teaching and research at Montana State University, offers students an excellent liberal arts and sciences education in nearly 50 majors, 25 minors and over 25 graduate degrees within the four areas of the humanities, natural sciences, mathematics and social sciences. Building on the great strength of our traditional disciplines, the college provides exciting opportunities for faculty and students at the cutting edge of interdisciplinary inquiry. L&S is dedicated to supporting student success, discovering and disseminating new knowledge, and providing an atmosphere in which discovery and learning are integrated and valued. L&S is at the confluence of intellectual tradition, discovery, and innovation. Posting Detail Information Number of Vacancies Multiple Positions Desired Start Date Review of applications will be on a continual basis and will continue until the position(s) are filled. Applicants will be notified if their application is being retained in the hiring pool for up to two years.* Position End Date (if temporary) Open Date Close Date Applications will be: Applications will be accepted and screened for the duration of this pool. Special Instructions Review of applications will be on a continual basis and will continue until the position(s) are filled. Applicants will be notified if their application is being retained in the hiring pool for up to two years. EEO Statement Montana State University values diverse perspectives and is committed to continually supporting, promoting and building an inclusive and culturally diverse campus environment. MSU recognizes the importance of work-life integration and strives to be responsive to the needs of dual career couples . Montana State University is committed to providing a working and learning environment free from discrimination. As such, the University does not discriminate in the admission, access to or conduct of its educational programs and activities nor in its employment policies and practices on the basis of an individual’s race, color, religion, national origin, creed, service in the uniformed services (as defined in state and federal law), veteran’s status, sex, age, political ideas, marital or family status, pregnancy, physical or mental disability, genetic information, gender identity, gender expression, or sexual orientation. In support of the University’s mission to be inclusive and diverse, applications from qualified minorities, women, veterans and persons with disabilities are highly encouraged. Montana State University makes accommodation for any known disability that may interfere with an applicant’s ability to compete in the hiring process or an employee’s ability to perform the duties of the job. To request an accommodation, contact the Human Resources Office, PO Box 172520, Montana State University, Bozeman MT 59717-2520; 406-994-3651; recruitment@montana.edu . In compliance with the Montana Veteran’s Employment Preference Act, MSU provides preference in employment to veterans, disabled veterans, and certain eligible relatives of veterans. To claim veteran’s preference please complete the veteran’s preference information located in the Demographics section of your profile. MSU ’s Non-Discrimination Policy and Discrimination Grievance Procedures can be located on the MSU Website: http://www.montana.edu/hr/disability_accommodation/complaints_grievances.html . FLSA Details FLSA Determination FLSA Status Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume OR Curriculum Vitae Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). University Human Resources 920 Technology BLVD, Suite A Bozeman, MT 59718 406-994-3651 humanresources@montana.edu Accessibility | Contact List | Directories | Jobs | Legal & Privacy Policy | Site Index Montana State University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.montana.edu/postings/50288": {
      "title": "Mechanical & Industrial Engineering Non-Tenure Track Faculty (Applicant Pool)",
      "description": "Montana State University Employment Opportunities | Mechanical &amp; Industrial Engineering Non-Tenure Track Faculty (Applicant Pool) Skip to Main Content Jobs at Montana State University Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About MSU About Bozeman Benefits Academics Mechanical & Industrial Engineering Non-Tenure Track Faculty (Applicant Pool) Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. This position is not eligible for NEW sponsorship. Review of applications will be on a continual basis and will continue until the position(s) are filled. Applicants are retained in the hiring pool for up to two years. Position Details Position Information Announcement Number FAC - VA - 26088 For questions regarding this position, please contact: Richard Owens 406-994-6755 richard.owens2@montana.edu Classification Title Instructor Working Title Mechanical & Industrial Engineering Non-Tenure Track Faculty (Applicant Pool) Brief Position Overview Mechanical & Industrial Engineering faculty members are expected to contribute to excellence in undergraduate and graduate teaching in areas of mechanical engineering, mechanical engineering technology or industrial engineering related to the applicant’s strengths. Other responsibilities include advising and mentoring undergraduate students, participation in accreditation activities and committee assignments, and service to the university, state, and profession. Faculty Tenure Track No Faculty Rank Non-Tenure Track Position Number 4A6135 Department Mechanical & Industrial Engineering Division College of Engineering Appointment Type Non-Tenure Track (Pooled) Contract Term Semester Semester Fall or Spring If other, specify From date If other, specify End date Union Affiliation Contact HR for Determination FTE Varies Benefits Eligible Depends on FTE (please contact Human Resources - 406-994-3651) Salary Salary commensurate with experience, education, and qualifications Contract Type LOA If other, please specify Recruitment Type Open Position Details General Statement Montana State University seeks qualified individuals to provide non-tenurable level instruction and/or related functions. Instructors are needed to teach classes depending upon need and available funding. Applications are accepted on a continual basis (unless otherwise indicated) and will be maintained in pool for two years. After two years, candidates will be notified to re-submit materials for continued consideration. The department or program search committees or hiring authority will review and evaluate the submitted application materials. Applicants will be notified if their application is being retained in the non-tenure track faculty hiring pool. Appointments are on a part-time basis and vary in duration, a couple weeks, a semester or an academic year, depending on the specific needs of the department/program. Salary is dependent on number of courses taught and duration. Duties and Responsibilities Duties and Responsibilities Include: Teach on-campus and/or online courses. Advise, evaluate and assist students. Participate in curriculum and/or course development. Contribute positively and professionally to the academic success of students and the mission of Montana State University. Required Qualifications – Experience, Education, Knowledge & Skills Relevant degree and experience in Mechanical Engineering, Mechanical Engineering Technology, Industrial Engineering or related field. Preferred Qualifications – Experience, Education, Knowledge & Skills Relevant professional experience and insight into the theoretical frameworks of the field or discipline of teaching responsibility. Currency in area of teaching responsibility. Previous teaching experience. The Successful Candidate Will Have a broad understanding in the fields of Mechanical Engineering, Mechanical Engineering Technology or Industrial Engineering. Have the ability to mentor and inspire students from all backgrounds. Possess excellent written and oral communication skills. Have the ability to establish and maintain positive working relationships with colleagues, students, and the engineering industry. Have the potential to provide leadership and to assume responsibility for organizational and program activities. Position Special Requirements/Additional Information The position for which you have applied MAY BE a Union position. Union affiliation is determined by job duties and the collective bargaining unit. Non-Tenure Track Faculty – Any position that is .5 FTE or more is represented by the Associated Faculty of Montana State University ( AFMSU ). Faculty covered by AFMSU may choose to pay membership dues. This is a condition of employment. Additional information can be found at AFMSU or by emailing AFSMU . Any position less than .5 FTE is not represented by the Union and is not required to pay dues. If the FTE changes during employment, the representational status of this position could be impacted. Exceptions: Anyone having successfully completed a PEI exam or any faculty working in Extension are exempt from the Union. This job description should not be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities or requirements, but a general description of the job. Nothing contained herein restricts Montana State University’s rights to assign or reassign duties and responsibilities to this job at any time. Physical Demands To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily with or without reasonable accommodations. The requirements listed above are representative of the knowledge, skill, and/or ability required. The Program The Department The Mechanical and Industrial Engineering (M&IE) Department offers ABET accredited BS degrees in Mechanical Engineering, Mechanical Engineering Technology and Industrial Engineering. Graduate offerings include MS degrees in Industrial & Management Engineering and Mechanical Engineering, and a Ph.D. degree in Engineering with options in Industrial or Mechanical Engineering. The Department is strong in both traditional and emerging areas of each discipline, with substantial laboratory facilities supporting instructional and research missions. Available to M&IE Department faculty are collaborations with MSU’s Energy Research Institute, Center for Biofilm Engineering, Imaging and Chemical Analysis Laboratory, Magnetic Resonance Lab, Subzero Science and Engineering Research Facility, Montana Microfabrication Facility, Western Transportation Institute, Wind Application Center, Montana Manufacturing Extension Center, Thermal Biology Institute, and Center for Bio-Inspired Nano-Materials. The College The Norm Asbjornson College of Engineering comprises five academic departments (Chemical & Biological Engineering, Civil Engineering, Gianforte School of Computing, Electrical & Computer Engineering, and Mechanical & Industrial Engineering and is home to the following research centers: Center for Biofilm Engineering ( CBE ), Western Transportation Institute ( WTI ), Montana Microfabrication Facility ( MMF ) and the Subzero Science and Engineering Research Facility; the Montana Manufacturing Extension Center ( MMEC ); and the Army and Air Force ROTC Units. The College enrolls approximately 4,000 students with more than 200 studying in graduate degree programs. Doctoral degree programs include a Ph.D. in Computer Science and a Ph.D. in Engineering with options in the fields of Applied Mechanics, Chemical Engineering, Civil Engineering, Electrical & Computer Engineering, Environmental Engineering, Industrial Engineering, and Mechanical Engineering. The College is excited that Asbjornson Hall opened in 2019. This innovative facility is transforming education and scholarly activity at MSU . Posting Detail Information Number of Vacancies Multiple Positions Desired Start Date Review of applications will be on a continual basis and will continue until the position(s) are filled. Applicants will be notified if their application is being retained in the hiring pool for up to two years. Position End Date (if temporary) Open Date Close Date Applications will be: Applications will be accepted and screened for the duration of this pool. Special Instructions This position is not eligible for NEW sponsorship. Review of applications will be on a continual basis and will continue until the position(s) are filled. Applicants are retained in the hiring pool for up to two years. EEO Statement Montana State University is an equal opportunity employer. MSU does not discriminate against any applicant on the basis of race, color, religion, creed, political ideas, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, age, marital status, national origin, physical or mental disability, or any other protected class status in violation of any applicable law. In compliance with the Montana Veteran’s Employment Preference Act, MSU provides preference in employment to veterans, disabled veterans, and certain eligible relatives of veterans. To claim veteran’s preference, please complete the veteran’s preference information located in the Demographics section of your profile. FLSA Details FLSA Determination FLSA Status Exempt Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Resume OR Curriculum Vitae Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). University Human Resources 920 Technology BLVD, Suite A Bozeman, MT 59718 406-994-3651 humanresources@montana.edu Accessibility | Contact List | Directories | Jobs | Legal & Privacy Policy | Site Index Montana State University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
